// **The `Sort` module** (ADR-0104): ordering a view in place, for any element type, given a comparison. W7's
// second sub-wave, the project's **third** module, and the first library code that is *polymorphic* — so the
// first that depends on W5 rather than merely coexisting with it.
//
// **Three things were probed before a line was written**, because the whole module rests on them and all three
// hold: a `[]T` **view parameter is mutable** through the callee (so an in-place sort can exist), a `$T`
// parameter **infers through a view** (`xs: []$T`, ADR-0084), and a **procedure pointer** can be passed and
// called (ADR-0059). Writing the module first and finding one missing would have meant designing around a gap
// instead of knowing there was none.
//
// **Why the caller supplies the comparison.** `sort(xs, less)` takes a procedure rather than requiring `<` on
// the element type, and that is a language fact rather than a taste: resolving an **operator** inside a `$T`
// template against the instantiated type is a lookup instantiation does not do. `operator <` exists (ADR-0048)
// and `#modify` can *reject* an instantiation (ADR-0095), but nothing can *select* an implementation per
// instantiated type — that would be operator-bounded polymorphism, a real feature belonging to whichever wave
// decides how a template states its requirements. A comparison parameter is also the only form that serves a
// scalar **and** a struct with nothing the language lacks, and it composes with `String.compare`, which the
// previous sub-wave shaped for exactly this.
//
// **Why insertion sort, which is not a performance argument.** It is `O(n²)`, said plainly. The reasons to
// choose it here are that it is **stable** (equal elements keep their order, which quicksort does not give),
// that it needs **no extra storage** (a merge sort would allocate, and allocation is what ADR-0103 §3 declined
// to decide), and that it is **short enough to read**, which matters for the first sorting routine in a
// language whose test suite compares two independent engines. A faster algorithm is a later decision with a
// benchmark behind it; W8 owns performance.
//
// **Why the calls here are the `_ints` wrappers.** Cross-file instantiation is deferred (ADR-0082 §5), so this
// file *cannot* call `sort(a[], less_int)` — that is E0268, and `type-errors/072` pins it. Writing this module
// is what found that the refusal did not exist: a `$T` parameter's type is `PoolId::ERROR` and `ERROR` matches
// anything, so the call type-checked and the missing instantiation leaked out of an engine as "no routine for
// file 2 proc 0". So `sort_ints` and `ints_sorted` are not conveniences today — they are the only way an
// importer can use this module, because the wrapper lives where the instantiation can happen.
//
// The same work found a second leak: passing an **imported procedure as a value** — `less_int` handed to a
// procedure-pointer parameter — reported "this compiler has a gap; please report it" for a program the language
// allows. `ImportedProcs` had already resolved the name to a `ProcRef` and a `DeclId` carries a file id, so the
// value was representable all along and what was missing was a three-line bridge (ADR-0104 §1).
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * a **reversed** array sorted ascending, which is insertion sort's worst case and the one an accidentally
//     correct implementation is least likely to get right;
//   * **duplicates**, so stability has something to preserve, and the count is checked after so no element is
//     lost or duplicated by the shifting;
//   * an **already sorted** array, which must come out unchanged;
//   * a **single element** and an **empty** view, both of which `is_sorted` calls ordered because there is no
//     adjacent pair to violate;
//   * `ints_sorted` disagreeing with an unsorted array, so the postcondition check is not vacuously true —
//     without this, a sort that did nothing would pass every other assertion here.
//
// The exit code is **63**, six independent groups each contributing one bit, so a routine that returned a
// constant could not pass: the corpus differential compares the two engines to each other, so a wrong answer
// both give is invisible unless the *value* is asserted.
#import "Basic";
#import "Sort";

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // A reversed array — insertion sort's worst case, and the case that actually exercises the shifting.
    a: [5]s64;
    a[0] = 5;
    a[1] = 4;
    a[2] = 3;
    a[3] = 2;
    a[4] = 1;
    sort_ints(a[]);
    if a[0] == 1 && a[1] == 2 && a[2] == 3 && a[3] == 4 && a[4] == 5 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if ints_sorted(a[]) {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // Duplicates, so stability has something to preserve — and so a lost or repeated element shows up.
    b: [6]s64;
    b[0] = 3;
    b[1] = 1;
    b[2] = 3;
    b[3] = 2;
    b[4] = 1;
    b[5] = 2;
    sort_ints(b[]);
    if b[0] == 1 && b[1] == 1 && b[2] == 2 && b[3] == 2 && b[4] == 3 && b[5] == 3 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // Already sorted: it must come out unchanged rather than merely ordered.
    c: [3]s64;
    c[0] = 7;
    c[1] = 8;
    c[2] = 9;
    sort_ints(c[]);
    if c[0] == 7 && c[1] == 8 && c[2] == 9 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // One element, which the loop never enters, and which is ordered.
    d: [1]s64;
    d[0] = 42;
    sort_ints(d[]);
    if d[0] == 42 && ints_sorted(d[]) {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // **The check that keeps the rest honest**: an unsorted view must be reported unsorted. Without it, a
    // `sort` that did nothing at all would satisfy every assertion above that only reads a sorted array.
    e: [3]s64;
    e[0] = 2;
    e[1] = 1;
    e[2] = 3;
    if !ints_sorted(e[]) {
        n = n + 32;
    }

    exit(n);
}
